# The hard part of chords is changing between them, and a change is only as hard as the fingers
# that move. A minor to F keeps finger 2 where it is, which is why the pair is taught together.
lesson music-uke-chord-change v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=5 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Changing chords: the finger that stays"
  goal "Find the finger that stays down between two chords, and change from A minor to F."

  look {
    scene 36x15 {
      row both at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        chordbox am tuning=uke chord=Am frets=3 show=fingers name=true letters=true lit=[]
        chordchange change tuning=uke from=Am to=F frets=3
        chordbox f tuning=uke chord=F frets=3 show=fingers name=true letters=true lit=[]
      }
    }
    say """
      From A minor to F, finger 2 stays on the G string and finger 1 goes down. The middle card
      rings the finger that stays.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.uke-f-fingers
    show music.uke-finger-stays
    show music.uke-play-f
    level easy {
      worked music.change-how-many-stay k=3 level=medium
    }
    practice music.change-how-many-stay count=2 seed=3 hard-seed=14
    practice music.change-new-or-lift count=1 seed=2
    practice music.change-from-cards count=1 seed=5
    show music.uke-play-dm
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.change-easier k=3 s=1 easy-k=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.change-middle t=0 p=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Keep down the finger that stays. Move only the others."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Time one minute of changing between A minor and F, counting the changes, and write the number
    down each day. The count going up is the progress, and the child can see it. Slow and clean
    beats fast and buzzy.

    Without the change card, a finger stays only if the same finger is on the same string at the
    same fret in both chords; G7 and D minor share a place that two different fingers use, which is
    the trap in one version. The two-star question asks for a reason as well as a choice, so listen
    for "more fingers stay". The three-star question can be solved by trying pairs of cards.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the F chord and the finger that stays are as written, G7 to D minor is counted once in the teacher's pen before the changes between one- and two-finger chords, a finger goes down new, the cards share a finger in the same place, D minor is as written, the two-star starts from F, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the F chord and the finger that stays are as written, the changes are between two- and three-finger chords, G7 to D minor among them, a finger lifts off, the cards share no finger or a place two different fingers use, D minor is as written, and the two-star and three-star are as written; there is no second change to compare at this level, because the two-star's other versions would show the same three cards swapped."
  }
}
